  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is reverberation?",
      "a": "Reverberation is sound lingering after it is made, because of many reflections bouncing off the walls and arriving one after another. It makes a voice or instrument sound fuller.",
      "asks": [
        "What does reverb mean?",
        "Why does sound linger in a hall?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a bathroom echo?",
      "a": "Bathrooms are full of hard, smooth surfaces like tiles, glass and porcelain, which reflect sound instead of absorbing it. The reflections keep bouncing around the small room.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are bathrooms so loud?",
        "Why do tiles reflect sound?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I sound better singing in the shower?",
      "a": "Reflections off hard walls add reverberation, which smooths over small mistakes and makes notes last longer. The small space also boosts some lower notes, so your voice sounds richer and louder.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my voice sound good in the bathroom?",
        "Is shower singing really better?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do concert halls sound good?",
      "a": "Concert halls are designed so sound reflects from walls and ceiling in a controlled way, giving a pleasant amount of reverberation, often around two seconds, without confusing echoes.",
      "asks": [
        "How are concert halls designed?",
        "What makes good acoustics?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an empty room sound different from a furnished one?",
      "a": "An empty room has bare, hard walls and floors that reflect sound, so it sounds echoey. Furniture, curtains and carpets absorb sound and make the room quieter and less boomy.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my room echo when it is empty?",
        "Why do curtains make a room quieter?"
      ]
